    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d648-a5">

                <pb facs="1453" n="1339" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg1339"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure 6]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to the British Foreign Secretary</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Eden</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">most secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quebec</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-09-13">September 13, 1943</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">
                        Welfare No</hi>. —. Most Secret and
                    Personal.</p>
                <p>Reference 
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Welfare</hi>
                     677.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d648-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>It is agreed that Count Sforza’s visit to the Mediterranean<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d648-f2"/> is put off till further notice and that we shall be
                    consulted again.<anchor n="8" xml:id="d648-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>13.9.43</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d648-f1" n="6" xml:id="d648-f1-content">
                <p>Not found in United States files.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d648-f2" n="7" xml:id="d648-f2-content">
                <p>Count Carlo Sforza, an Italian anti-Fascist leader then in the United States, had
                    been informed by Assistant Secretary of State Adolf A. Berle, Jr., on September
                    2, 1943, that “we would be glad to endeavor to arrange his passage to North
                    Africa.” See <ref target="frus1943v02#pg_402">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1943, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 402</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d648-f3" n="8" xml:id="d648-f3-content">
                <p>The British Embassy at Washington notified the Department of State on September
                    25, 1943, that the British Foreign Office then felt that Sforza should proceed
                    to Europe as soon as possible.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
